{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/832852-using-different-difficulties-for-different-weapons","id":832852,"title":"Using different difficulties for different weapons.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-02-18T17:17:53-08:00","id":832852,"title":"Using different difficulties for different weapons.","content":"in vampire, the weapons don't have a difficulty. Assume 6. Also, depending on what version you're playing, the highest a melee weapon will add to your damage is +4 with a greatsword (though some go for the full +6) <br /> <br /> In werewolf and mage, everything's everywhere. Daggers are difficulty 4, larger weapons can go to 7 or even 8. Dodge in werewolf is at 5 (not sure on mage) and all in all- It's everywhere. <br /> (mage20 also goes all out and gives us rules for big guns and explosives. That was nice of them.) Unlike vampire, we're given far,far more deadly weapons in mage (yep, In vampire you could get the super-damaging weapons, it's just that it's harder to ask your GM for things when it's not in the rulebook.) <br /> <br /> <br /> Anyhow; What's better? The basic system for vampire or the more complex thing for mage and werewolf. <br /> (personally, I care more for ballance than simplification, but I've not really ran both so...)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7244-innocent-flower","name":"Innocent Flower","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7244_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"322"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-18T17:32:48-08:00","id":832864,"title":"","content":"Go with what ever you feel comfortable with, if you have never ran before you might want to keep it as simple as possible.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3953-dwight","name":"Dwight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3953_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2355"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-18T18:48:18-08:00","id":832932,"title":"","content":"I vastly prefer keeping things to VtM's default difficulty of 6. Though for the dodging thing, that's usually supposed to be variable, which makes sense; there should be a penalty for trying to dodge when you're in a situation that limits movement.<br /> <br /> As I've pointed out in the recent -1 diff or +1 dice thread, difficulty modifiers are generally better, and scale more with larger dice-pools. This creates really weird situations regarding what the most &quot;deadly&quot; weapons really are.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-19T09:58:48-08:00","id":833358,"title":"","content":"I tend to favour a flat Difficulty of 6. Mainly because I feel it makes it easier to incorporate unusual or improvised weapons.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1666-firstborn8","name":"Firstborn8","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"16"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}